# Codename and attack rules

These rules apply to every character. Codenames identify animations, attacks and attack-created objects consistently across character definitions, assets, combat, controls, AI and UI metadata. A character's mechanics and player-facing move names remain character-specific; the internal naming system is shared.

## Character IDs and animation filenames

Each character has a four-digit ID, stored as a string with leading zeroes. Use the character's own ID wherever <id> appears below.

The standard asset path is:

assets/characters/<id>/<id>_<animation>_<frame>.png

Use lowercase codenames and underscores. Numbered frames start at 1 and play in numeric order. The final number identifies the animation frame, never the attack number or an object instance. A single-frame animation still ends in _1.

| Animation | Filename pattern |
| --- | --- |
| Idle | <id>_idle_<frame>.png |
| Run | <id>_run_<frame>.png |
| Jump | <id>_jump_<frame>.png |
| Fall | <id>_fall_<frame>.png |
| Land | <id>_land_<frame>.png |
| Hurt | <id>_hurt_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air hurt | <id>_midair_hurt_<frame>.png |
| Shield preparation | <id>_prepshield_<frame>.png |
| Shield hold | <id>_shielding_<frame>.png |
| Shield release | <id>_releaseshield_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air shield | <id>_midair_shielding_<frame>.png |
| Movement / dash | <id>_mouvment_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air movement | <id>_midair_mouvment_<frame>.png |
| Numbered attack | <id>_attackN_<frame>.png |
| Summon startup (the owner's own pose before its summon appears) | <id>_attackN_summon_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air numbered attack | <id>_midair_attackN_<frame>.png |
| Extra attack | <id>_extra_attack_<frame>.png |

Replace N with the attack number, from 1 through 5. Preserve the codename spelling mouvment for animation keys and assets, including midair_mouvment.

## Numbered attacks and airborne versions

A character has at least 2 and at most 5 numbered attack families: attack1 through attack5. Numbers must be sequential, with no gaps. Ground and mid-air versions belong to the same attack family, and summons and techniques count toward the numbered total.

Every ordinary numbered attack must have a functional midair_attackN definition and animation. A summon or technique, which is ground-only, is exempt from this requirement. An ordinary attack5 always requires midair_attack5.

Attack numbers identify moves; they do not prescribe damage, effects or mechanics. A numbered attack may be a direct strike, projectile, summon, deployable or another character-specific ability.

extra_attack is a separate, additional character-specific action. It may be a projectile, throw, utility move or other unique attack. It does not count toward the 2–5 numbered attack limit, so a character may have five numbered attacks plus extra_attack. Its airborne availability is defined by the character's loadout.

Shield, movement, jump, down and transform are not numbered attacks.

## Attack launch levels

Use the following launch levels according to the attack's impact:

| Launch level | Attack impact |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | Light attacks |
| 2 | Medium attacks |
| 3 | Big-impact attacks |

Launch levels describe attack impact independently of numbered attack codenames.

## Attack-button routing

Every numbered attack a character has is a numbered button of its own, pressed directly on keyboard, gamepad and touch. No held stance, modifier or other button reaches a numbered attack or changes which attack a button triggers.

| Numbered attack count | Numbered buttons |
| --- | --- |
| 2 | attack1, attack2 |
| 3 | attack1, attack2, attack3 |
| 4 | attack1, attack2, attack3, attack4 |
| 5 | attack1, attack2, attack3, attack4, attack5 |

Each numbered button is one of three kinds of move, set by the character's loadout:

| Kind | Loadout entry | Mid-air version |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ordinary attack | { ground: 'attackN', air: 'midair_attackN' } | Required |
| Summon | { type: 'summon', id: 'attackN' } | None (ground-only) |
| Technique | { type: 'technique', id: 'attackN' } | None (ground-only) |

attack1 and attack2 are always ordinary attacks. attack3, attack4 and attack5 may each be any of the three. A summon or technique is keyed by the button it is and has its own cooldown. Whether attack3 or attack4 is an ordinary attack, a summon or a technique depends on the loadout, not its codename.

For example, attack3 may be an ordinary third attack with midair_attack3 on one character, and a summon on its own Attack 3 button with no mid-air version on another. Character 0001 has four buttons: attack1 and attack2 are ordinary attacks, attack3 is the clone summon and attack4 the Sphere Rush technique.

A summon or technique starts only on its own button's press, on the ground, with the fighter free to act and its cooldown over. Otherwise the press does nothing: no other attack in its place and nothing kept for later.

A summon may have a startup: the owner's own summoning pose, its attackN_summon animation, named in the summon as startupAnimation. It plays once from the accepted press, the owner committed to it (standing still, facing kept, no other action), and the summon appears as it ends. A hit, lost ground or a target gone from play cuts it short with no summon. The cooldown starts on the press either way. Character 0001's attack3 has one: 0001_attack3_summon_1 through 0001_attack3_summon_4, then its clone.

down is a directional input only: the fast fall and launch steering. It never changes what a button does. Menus keep their own Down navigation.

Controls, AI, UI labels, cooldowns and combat must agree on the same loadout and routing.

## Mid-air shield

Characters with mid-air shield frames use midair_shielding for their airborne shield.

Characters with no mid-air shield frames have no airborne shield: the shield button does nothing in mid-air. There is no mid-air dodge.

## Attack-created objects

Projectiles, clones, summons, deployables and attack-owned effects use the codename of the attack that creates them.

The standard object filename is:

<id>_<attackCodename>_object_<frame>.png

| Owning attack | Object filename pattern |
| --- | --- |
| attackN | <id>_attackN_object_<frame>.png |
| midair_attackN | <id>_midair_attackN_object_<frame>.png |
| extra_attack | <id>_extra_attack_object_<frame>.png |

The object codename records gameplay ownership rather than a fictional technique or display name. Runtime instance IDs may distinguish concurrent objects, but must not change the asset frame suffix.

If an attack creates multiple visually distinct object types, use documented child keys beneath its object namespace, such as attackN_object_<type>_<frame>. Do not use frame numbers to distinguish object types.

A summon may explicitly reuse its owner's animation clips and hit data. Shared art does not need duplicate PNGs; summon-owned art and effects still follow the creating attack's object namespace. The owner's summoning pose is the owner's art, not the summon's: it is attackN_summon, while the summoned entity's effects are attackN_object.

## Internal codenames and display names

Use attack1 through attack5, midair_attack1 through midair_attack5 and extra_attack as the shared internal vocabulary. Do not introduce alternate internal move names based on a character, technique or player-facing label.

Legacy terms such as ba, maba, cba and uniqueba are not live attack IDs. They may appear only when documenting history or reading older saved data for migration.

Player-facing names such as “Punch,” “Kick” or other character-specific move names may appear in menus and descriptions. They do not replace canonical IDs in character definitions, animation keys, filenames, controls, AI, cooldowns or object references.

Touch buttons show a frame of the character's own art for their move, chosen in its UI data by animation codename and frame (counted from 0), never by a file name of their own. The picture is presentation only: each button still sends its control codename.

When adding or updating a character, keep its attack count, button routing, airborne variants, object ownership and animation assets consistent with these shared rules.
